//Que: Reverse the words of given string
//Program

public class Main {
    public static void main(String args[]) {
        String s1 = "nagpur is in maharashtra";
        if (s1.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("String is empty");
        } else {
            String[] s2 = s1.trim().split("\\s+"); // Split into words
            for (int i = s2.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) { // Traverse in reverse
                System.out.print(s2[i] + " ");
            }
        }
    }
}
Explanation:
split("\\s+"): Splits the string into an array of words.
Loop: Iterates through the array in reverse order and prints each word.

Time Complexity: O(n)
split("\\s+"): O(n), where n is the length of the string.
Loop: O(m), where m is the number of words (usually much smaller than n).

Space Complexity: O(n)
The split() method creates an array of size proportional to the input string.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1: Understand the Problem
The goal is to reverse the order of words in a string.
Words are separated by one or more spaces.
Edge cases:
Empty string.
String with only spaces.
Leading/trailing spaces.
Multiple spaces between words.

Step 2: Think of Solutions
Using split("\\s+") and Loop:
Split the string into an array of words.
Traverse the array in reverse order and print the words.
Using StringBuilder:
Split the string into words.
Append words to a StringBuilder in reverse order.
Using a Stack:
Push words onto a stack and pop them to reverse the order.

Step 3: Choose the Best Solution
split("\\s+") and Loop is simple and efficient.
StringBuilder is more efficient for concatenation.
Stack is useful for learning but less efficient for this problem.

